This chapter proves the second half of the main theorem.
For a nonempty arrangement $`\mathcal{P}` of hyperplanes in $`\mathbb{P}^n_k`, the cohomology of the complement $`U_{\mathcal{P}}` is a canonical subalgebra of the Orlik–Solomon algebra of the central cone $`c\mathcal{P}`.

The idea is short.
The complement $`V_{\mathcal{P}}` of the cone maps to $`U_{\mathcal{P}}`, and the affine theorem computes its cohomology, with a generator $`[\ell_i]` for each equation.
The functions $`\ell_i` live on the cone complement, and their ratios $`\ell_i/\ell_j` are units on $`U_{\mathcal{P}}`.
Pullback of the logarithm of each ratio is $`[\ell_i]-[\ell_j]`.
So the subalgebra to look for is the one generated by differences of generators, and it turns out to be the kernel of the boundary $`\partial`.

One could stop sooner.
Choosing a hyperplane $`H_0` to put at infinity makes $`U_{\mathcal{P}}` the complement of an affine arrangement, the decone, so the affine theorem already computes its cohomology.
But the resulting presentation depends on $`H_0`.
The point of this chapter is an answer that does not, and that requires care on both sides: neither the space, nor the algebra, nor the map may be defined using a hyperplane at infinity.
The hyperplane appears only inside proofs.

# What the complement is

The theorem says that a map is independent of the hyperplane at infinity, and that only makes sense if its target is defined without one.
So the coordinate ring of $`U_{\mathcal{P}}` cannot be defined as the ring of a decone.

Let $`W` be the vector space of dimension $`n+1` whose projectivization carries the arrangement.
The ring of the cone complement is the symmetric algebra of $`W^*` with the equations inverted, which is graded by degree.
The ring of $`U_{\mathcal{P}}` is its degree-zero part: the fractions $`a/b` of elements of the same degree, with $`b` a product of equations.
This is Mathlib's homogeneous localization, and it involves no hyperplane at infinity and no choice of equations.
Its inclusion in the ring of the cone complement is pullback along $`V_{\mathcal{P}}\to U_{\mathcal{P}}`, the map {name OrlikSolomon.SmoothAlg.projectiveToCone}`projectiveToCone`, and everything below is built on this one canonical map.

*Why this is the right object.*
The library proves that the spectrum of this ring is the complement of the hyperplanes in projective space.
{name OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveArrangement.complementOpenIso}`complementOpenIso` identifies it with the open subscheme of Mathlib's $`\operatorname{Proj}\operatorname{Sym}(W^*)` that {name OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveArrangement.mem_complementOpen_iff}`mem_complementOpen_iff` describes as the points on no hyperplane, and {name OrlikSolomon.projIsoMvPolynomial}`projIsoMvPolynomial` identifies that scheme with $`\operatorname{Proj}k[x_0,\ldots,x_n]` in a basis.
This comparison certifies that the object is what the statement means.
The proof of the structure theorem does not use it, or schemes at all: no other proof module imports the module that contains it.
The planned {ref "geometric"}[refactor on schemes] would build its projective complements on this comparison, and none of it is formalized.

*A tempting alternative that is wrong.*
One could try the subalgebra of the ring of the cone complement generated by the ratios $`\ell_i/\ell_j` of equations.
For one hyperplane in dimension at least two, these ratios generate only the constants, while the complement is an affine space of dimension $`n`, as {name OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveArrangement.ComplementRing.ratio_notMem_adjoin_ratioUnit}`ratio_notMem_adjoin_ratioUnit` shows.
The numerators must be all linear forms: the ring is generated by the ratios $`\xi/\ell` of a linear form and an equation, which is a theorem here, {name OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveArrangement.ComplementRing.range_toCone_eq_adjoin}`range_toCone_eq_adjoin`, and not the definition.

*The empty arrangement.*
It is excluded, by the hypothesis `[Nonempty P]` on every statement about the complement.
There is no hyperplane to put at infinity, the space is $`\mathbb{P}^n` itself, and its cohomology needs a projective bundle formula that the axioms do not provide.
The ring above would not be the right one either: for empty $`\mathcal{P}` it is $`k`, while $`\mathbb{P}^n` is not affine when $`n\ge1`.
No fake hyperplane at infinity is chosen to get around this.

*No rational points are needed.*
As in the affine case, everything is stated on coordinate rings.
The three rational points of $`\mathbb{P}^1` over $`\mathbb{F}_2` have a complement with no $`\mathbb{F}_2`-points, and its ring is nonzero.

