# Cone and decone

A hyperplane at infinity is a tool for proofs, and this section records what it provides.
Choose $`H_0\in\mathcal{P}` with equation $`\ell_0`.
Every point of the complement lies in the chart $`\ell_0\ne0`, which gives two isomorphisms.

The first says that $`U_{\mathcal{P}}` is the complement of the decone, the arrangement cut out on the slice $`\ell_0=1`.
The second says that the cone complement is a product,
$$`V_{\mathcal{P}}\simeq U_{\mathcal{P}}\times\mathbb{G}_m,\qquad v\longmapsto\bigl(v/\ell_0(v),\ell_0(v)\bigr),`
with inverse $`(u,t)\mapsto tu`, and that under it $`\ell_i=t(\ell_i/\ell_0)`.
So {bp "lem:log-mul"}[the identities of logarithms] give $`[\ell_i]=[t]+[\ell_i/\ell_0]`.
This is why differences of the classes $`[\ell_i]` come from $`U_{\mathcal{P}}`, and why $`[t]` is what is left over.

These are isomorphisms of coordinate rings, and the maps are inverse at that level, not merely on rational points.

:::node "lem:decone" (decl := OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveArrangement.ComplementRing.deconeEquiv) (decl := OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveArrangement.ComplementRing.deconeEquiv_ratio_mul) (decl := OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveArrangement.ComplementRing.coneEquiv) (decl := OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveArrangement.ComplementRing.coneEquiv_algebraMap_coord) (decl := OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveArrangement.ComplementRing.coneEquiv_toCone) (decl := OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveArrangement.ComplementRing.coneEquiv_symm_T) (decl := OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveArrangement.isHyperplane_comap_chart_slice) (decl := OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveArrangement.comap_chart_slice_inj) (decl := OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveArrangement.complementOpen_le_basicOpen) (decl := OrlikSolomon.SmoothAlg.deconeIso) (decl := OrlikSolomon.SmoothAlg.coneIso) (decl := OrlikSolomon.SmoothAlg.mapUnits_coneIso_hom_complementUnit)
The projective complement is the decone complement, and there is an isomorphism $`V_{\mathcal{P}}\simeq U_{\mathcal{P}}\times\mathbb{G}_m` given by $`v\mapsto(v/\ell_0(v),\ell_0(v))` with inverse $`(u,t)\mapsto tu`.
Under it, $`\ell_i=t(\ell_i/\ell_0)`.
Distinct hyperplanes other than $`H_0` restrict to distinct nonempty affine hyperplanes in the slice.

In the formalization these are isomorphisms of coordinate rings, and $`\mathbb{G}_m` is the Laurent polynomial ring.
:::

The next lemma gives the additive picture that goes with this product: $`C(V_{\mathcal{P}})` is two copies of $`C(U_{\mathcal{P}})`, the second shifted by $`(1,1)` and multiplied by $`[t]`.
It mirrors the decomposition of the algebra in the following sections.
The proof of the structure theorem below needs only its easy half, the section $`t=1`, which makes pullback injective.

:::node "lem:gm-product" (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.gmProductEquiv) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.gmProductEquiv_apply) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.gmProductEquiv_mem_grading_iff) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.map_laurentSection_gmProductEquiv) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.stdResidue_gmProductEquiv)
For suitable $`X`, with projection $`p:X\times\mathbb{G}_m\to X` and second coordinate $`t`, the map $`(b,c)\mapsto p^*b+[t]\,p^*c` is a $`B`-linear equivalence from $`C(X)\oplus C(X)` to $`C(X\times\mathbb{G}_m)`, with the second summand shifted by $`(1,1)`.
Pullback along the section $`t=1` recovers $`b`, and the residue recovers $`c`.

In the formalization, every smooth $`X` is suitable.
:::

# The central boundary

On the algebra side, the cone is a _central_ arrangement: its hyperplanes have a common point, so every intersection is nonempty.
So no empty-intersection relators occur, and all the relators are boundaries $`\partial e_I`.
This is what makes $`\partial` descend to the quotient.

On the exterior algebra, $`\partial` is the signed derivation that sends each generator to $`1`.
Terms that delete two letters cancel in pairs, which gives $`\partial^2=0`, so $`\partial` kills every relator.
The signed Leibniz rule then shows that it preserves the whole two-sided relation submodule, and {bp "lem:quotient-api"}[the linear universal property] gives the map on the quotient.
It is linear and not multiplicative, and it is a graded antiderivation, not a derivation in the unsigned sense.

The argument is specific to central arrangements.
For two distinct points of the affine line, the relation $`e_0e_1=0` holds, while the signed formula would make the boundary of that product $`e_1-e_0`, which is nonzero over a nonzero coefficient ring.
So no such operator exists there, as {name OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveAlgebraExamples.no_boundary_twoPoints}`no_boundary_twoPoints` shows.

:::node "lem:central-boundary" (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.boundary) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.boundary_relations) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.boundary_generator) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.boundary_boundary) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.boundary_word) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.boundary_mul_of_mem_bigrading) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.isHomogeneousOfShift_boundary)
For a central arrangement, the word boundary descends to a $`B`-linear map $`\partial` on the Orlik–Solomon algebra of bidegree $`(-1,-1)`, with $`\partial e_i=1`, $`\partial^2=0` and $`\partial(xy)=\partial(x)y+(-1)^px\partial(y)` for $`x` of cohomological degree $`p`.

Centrality here means that the hyperplanes have a common point, so it is independent of the origin used to identify an affine space with a vector space.
The boundary is defined over every commutative coefficient ring, including characteristic two and the zero ring.
:::

# The projective algebra

Define $`\overline{\operatorname{OS}}_B(\mathcal{P})=\ker\partial`, inside $`A=\operatorname{OS}_B(c\mathcal{P})`.
This definition chooses nothing, and the signed Leibniz rule shows that the kernel is a subalgebra.

Now choose any generator $`e_0`.
Every element of $`A` decomposes as
$$`a=(a-e_0\partial a)+e_0\partial a,`
and both $`a-e_0\partial a` and $`\partial a` lie in the kernel.
Applying $`\partial` to $`b+e_0c`, with $`b` and $`c` in the kernel, returns $`c`, so the decomposition is unique.
Rewriting each $`e_i` in a word as $`(e_i-e_0)+e_0` shows that the kernel is generated by the differences $`e_i-e_0`.

*Why this is a statement about modules.*
The decomposition looks like $`A\simeq\overline{\operatorname{OS}}_B(\mathcal{P})\otimes\Lambda[e_0]`, and it is tempting to state it as an isomorphism of algebras.
An ordinary tensor product of algebras would be wrong, because it makes the two factors commute.
Here $`e_0` anticommutes with the odd elements of the kernel: for instance $`e_0(e_1-e_0)=e_0e_1=-(e_1-e_0)e_0`.
The multiplicative form is a _graded_ tensor product, with the Koszul sign.
The proof needs only the module decomposition, so that is what the library states: {name OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.projectiveDecomposition}`projectiveDecomposition` is an equivalence of $`B`-modules, and it asserts nothing about products.

:::node "lem:projective-algebra" (decl := OrlikSolomon.ProjectiveArrangement.cone_isCentral) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.projectiveAlgebra) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.mem_projectiveAlgebra_iff) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.projectiveAlgebra_eq_adjoin) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.projectiveBigrading) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.projectiveAlgebra_isHomogeneous) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.projectiveBigradingGradedAlgebra) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.projectiveDecomposition) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.projectiveDecomposition_apply) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.coe_projectiveProjection) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.coe_projectiveBoundary) (decl := OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra.projectiveDecompositionComponentEquiv)
For $`A=\operatorname{OS}_B(c\mathcal{P})`, define $`\overline{\operatorname{OS}}_B(\mathcal{P})=\ker\partial`.
It is a graded $`B`-subalgebra, generated by the differences $`e_i-e_0` for any chosen label $`0`, and $`(b,c)\mapsto b+e_0c` is a module equivalence from two copies of it to $`A`, with the second summand shifted by $`(1,1)`.

The algebraic statements apply to every central arrangement, without requiring projective geometry or a cohomology theory.
The inverse of the module equivalence is $`a\mapsto(a-e_0\partial a,\partial a)`, and the component equivalence records the shift on the inherited $`R`-submodule pieces of the weighted bigrading.
:::

# The map, with no hyperplane at infinity

We now have the two canonical maps of the following square, with no choice in either: the inclusion of the kernel, and the pullback $`\pi^*` along $`\pi:V_{\mathcal{P}}\to U_{\mathcal{P}}`.
The right-hand map $`\Phi_{c\mathcal{P}}` is the {bp "thm:affine"}[affine theorem] for the cone, and the map $`\overline{\Phi}_{\mathcal{P}}` is what we want.
$$`\begin{array}{ccc} \overline{\operatorname{OS}}_B(\mathcal{P}) & \hookrightarrow & \operatorname{OS}_B(c\mathcal{P}) \\ \downarrow{\scriptstyle\overline{\Phi}_{\mathcal{P}}} & & \downarrow{\scriptstyle\Phi_{c\mathcal{P}}} \\ C(U_{\mathcal{P}}) & \xrightarrow{\ \pi^*\ } & C(V_{\mathcal{P}}) \end{array}`

The construction has four steps.

 * *The pullback is injective.* Choose any hyperplane. The product decomposition gives the section $`t=1` of $`\pi`, which gives $`\pi^*` a left inverse. The statement mentions no hyperplane, although its proof uses one.
 * *The kernel lands in the image of the pullback.* The kernel is generated by differences, and $`\Phi_{c\mathcal{P}}(e_i-e_j)=[\ell_i]-[\ell_j]=\pi^*[\ell_i/\ell_j]`. The image of $`\pi^*` is a subalgebra, so it contains the image of the whole kernel.
 * *The map is defined by descent.* Since $`\pi^*` is injective, there is exactly one map $`\overline{\Phi}_{\mathcal{P}}` that makes the square commute. Its only inputs are the arrangement and the theory.
 * *It is bijective.* It is injective because $`\Phi_{c\mathcal{P}}` and $`\pi^*` are. For surjectivity, pull back to any decone. By {bp "thm:generation"}[the first induction], the cohomology of the decone is generated by the logarithms of equations of its hyperplanes. Those are the restrictions of the ratios $`\ell_i/\ell_0`, so each generator is the image of a difference.

So a hyperplane at infinity occurs only inside proofs, and the definition of the map uses none.
The independence that the theorem claims is then visible in three ways.
{name OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.projectiveRealization_unique}`projectiveRealization_unique` says that the map is determined by the square.
{name OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.toAlgHom_projectiveEquiv_unique}`toAlgHom_projectiveEquiv_unique` says that it is the unique algebra map sending differences to logarithms of ratios, for any choice of equations.
And {name OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.projectiveEquiv_eq_map_section}`projectiveEquiv_eq_map_section` gives an explicit formula in the chart of every hyperplane.
Scaling an equation changes nothing, because the class of a constant vanishes, which is {name OrlikSolomon.OrdinaryTheory.logClass_projectiveRatioUnit_eq_of_ker_eq}`logClass_projectiveRatioUnit_eq_of_ker_eq`.

# The structure theorem

:::statement "The projective structure theorem in Lean"
With $`k`, $`R`, $`B` and the theory `T` as on the first page, let `P` be a nonempty projective arrangement in the finite-dimensional space `W`.
The isomorphism, its bigrading in both directions, and its formula on differences are as follows.

```lean
example :
    projectiveAlgebra B P.cone_isCentral ≃ₐ[B]
      T.C.obj (projectiveComplement P) :=
  T.projectiveEquiv P

example :
    IsHomogeneousOfShift
      (projectiveBigrading B ℬ P.cone_isCentral)
      (T.grading (projectiveComplement P)) 0
      (T.projectiveEquiv P) :=
  T.isHomogeneousOfShift_projectiveEquiv P

example :
    IsHomogeneousOfShift
      (T.grading (projectiveComplement P))
      (projectiveBigrading B ℬ P.cone_isCentral) 0
      (T.projectiveEquiv P).symm :=
  T.isHomogeneousOfShift_projectiveEquiv_symm P

example {ℓ ℓ' : Module.Dual k W}
    (hℓ : LinearMap.ker ℓ ∈ P)
    (hℓ' : LinearMap.ker ℓ' ∈ P) :
    T.projectiveEquiv P
      (projectiveDifference B P.cone_isCentral
        (P.coneHyperplaneEquiv ⟨_, hℓ⟩)
        (P.coneHyperplaneEquiv ⟨_, hℓ'⟩)) =
      T.logClass (projectiveRatioUnit hℓ hℓ') :=
  T.projectiveEquiv_difference P hℓ hℓ'
```

The square commutes.

```lean
example (x : projectiveAlgebra B P.cone_isCentral) :
    T.C.map (projectiveToCone P) (T.projectiveEquiv P x) =
      T.affineEquiv P.cone x :=
  T.map_projectiveToCone_projectiveEquiv P x
```

The diagonal, with coefficients in $`R`, is as follows.

```lean
example :
    projectiveAlgebra R P.cone_isCentral ≃ₐ[R]
      ⨁ q : ℕ, diagonalGrading
        (T.grading (projectiveComplement P)) q :=
  T.projectiveDiagonalEquiv P
```
:::

The diagonal needs a word, because $`\ker\partial` over $`R` is not obviously related to $`\ker\partial` over $`B` when $`B` is not flat over $`R`.
Take $`y` in $`H^{q,q}(U_{\mathcal{P}})`.
Pull it back to the cone, and use {bp "cor:ordinary"}[the affine diagonal theorem] to find $`x` of degree $`q` in $`\operatorname{OS}_R(c\mathcal{P})` that realizes it.
It remains to show that $`\partial x=0`.
Extending coefficients to $`B` commutes with $`\partial`, and the image of $`x` in $`\operatorname{OS}_B(c\mathcal{P})` lies in the kernel, because it corresponds to the pullback of a class from $`U_{\mathcal{P}}`.
And extending coefficients is injective, without flatness, because $`R\to B` is split over $`R`.
So the injectivity of coefficient extension detects the vanishing of the boundary.

As in the affine case, all of this is conditional on the theory `T`, and it holds with coefficients in $`\mathbb{Z}/2` and in the zero ring.

